Results

Overview

We completed a 22-autosome Biological Transferability Atlas on 1000 Genomes GRCh38 genotypes. The analysis has two layers with different coverage: (1) a mechanism layer that predicts cross-ancestry GWAS discordance from AF, LD, and selection features on all 22 chromosomes; and (2) a PRS atlas / intervention layer that applies nine published PGS to individuals and tests portability-risk interventions on chromosomes with available score genotypes (chr8–22). Partial-16 (chr1–15 + 22) is retained as a sensitivity checkpoint.

1. Genome-wide master table

Per-chromosome feature, label, and master tables were built with the conservative pipeline (AF divergence, LD divergence, selection proxies, multi-source GWAS concordance labels). Chromosomes 16–21 were the final missing masters; after they completed, all 22 were concatenated under tag `genomewide`.

The genome-wide master contains 37,603,487 variant×trait rows and 9,439,818 unique variants across chromosomes 1–22. Global `split_variant` was reassigned with seed 719 so evaluation is not contaminated by chromosome-wise leakage. Associated variants (those with multi-ancestry GWAS support) form the modeling subset used below (~3.03M associated rows; positive rate for high I² ≈ 0.38).

Interpretation. This is the analysis unit for mechanism learning: each row is a variant×trait observation with population-genetic features and a concordance label. Row count is larger than unique variants because traits are stacked. Claims about “genome-wide mechanism” refer to this 22-chromosome table.

2. Predicting cross-ancestry discordance (mechanism)

2.1 Pooled evaluation

On associated variants, under the held-out variant split, HistGradientBoosting with the full AF_LD_SEL feature set achieved:

- AUROC 0.683 (95% bootstrap CI 0.682–0.685)
- AUPRC 0.583
- Balanced accuracy 0.592; F1 0.385; ECE 0.025

Permuted-feature negative controls stayed near chance (AUROC ≈ 0.47–0.52), so the signal is not an artifact of class imbalance or leakage through feature packaging.

Feature-group ablation (associated, split_variant, HGB):

| Feature group | AUROC | AUPRC | balACC |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| FST only | 0.576 | 0.429 | 0.500 |
| AF | 0.663 | 0.545 | 0.551 |
| LD | 0.656 | 0.544 | 0.560 |
| SEL | 0.667 | 0.555 | 0.561 |
| AF_LD | 0.680 | 0.579 | 0.584 |
| AF_SEL | 0.677 | 0.570 | 0.577 |
| LD_SEL | 0.682 | 0.585 | 0.584 |
| AF_LD_SEL | 0.687 | 0.590 | 0.592 |

Continuous I² regression followed the same pattern: AF_LD_SEL Spearman ρ = 0.351 (Pearson r = 0.392), better than any single block.

Interpretation. Population-genetic features predict which associated variants show high cross-ancestry heterogeneity, but pooled discrimination is moderate (~0.68–0.69), not clinical-grade. AF, LD, and SEL each carry information; combining them helps, with diminishing returns beyond pairwise blocks. The gap between FST-only (0.58) and AF/LD/SEL blocks (~0.66) shows that a single summary divergence statistic is a weak substitute for the fuller feature set. Moderate pooled AUROC is expected when labels mix traits, loci, and study designs into one table—local chromosomal structure is stronger (next section).

2.2 Per-chromosome replication

Training and evaluating AF_LD_SEL separately on each chromosome yielded mean AUROC 0.815 (range 0.784 on chr1 to 0.855 on chr21). Every chromosome exceeded 0.78.

Interpretation. Within-chromosome models are substantially stronger than the pooled model. That pattern indicates that much of the predictive signal is chromosome-local (LD context, regional allele-frequency structure, trait-locus density) and is diluted when all chromosomes are forced into one global decision surface. For the paper, report both: pooled AUROC as the conservative genome-wide headline, and per-chromosome mean as evidence that the mechanism replicates across the autosome. Do not treat 0.80 pooled as a failed target; treat ~0.69 pooled / ~0.81 per-chr as the empirical operating point of this label definition at genome-wide scale.

3. Pathway-level instability (Q2)

Pathway aggregation on the 22-chromosome table highlighted trait-specific unstable Reactome pathways. Examples of top associated mean I²:

- CAD: RHO GTPase / IQGAP and nephrin interaction pathways (mean I²_assoc ≈ 0.69); GLP1–insulin secretion and mitochondrial DNA replication also high.
- T2D: lipoxin / hepoxilin / maresin biosynthesis (≈ 0.74); TP53 cell-death transcription; BCR / cytosolic Ca²⁺ signaling.
- BMI: SMDT1 processing and mitochondrial Ca²⁺ transport (≈ 0.77); intestinal hexose absorption and beta-cell gene regulation.
- LDL: mitochondrial DNA replication and complement cascade (≈ 0.77–0.78); RAS signaling downstream of NF1; sphingolipid and nucleotide biosynthesis.

Interpretation. Instability is not uniformly sprinkled across the genome; it concentrates in pathways tied to lipid signaling, mitochondrial function, immune/complement activity, and GTPase cytoskeletal control—biology that is both trait-relevant and known to vary across human populations. Pathway tops are hypothesis generators for where additional multi-ancestry GWAS sampling would most improve transferability, not proof that those pathways cause PRS failure.

4. Score-shift atlas across superpopulations (Q1)

Nine published PGS (T2D, CAD, BMI, LDL) were scored on 3,202 unrelated 1000 Genomes individuals using chr8–22 score pgens. Superpopulation means were EUR-centered (δ_EUR).

Largest mean |δ_EUR| (non-EUR):

| PGS | Trait | Mean \|δ_EUR\| |
|---|---|---:|
| PGS000014 | CAD | 2.85 |
| PGS004696 | CAD | 2.75 |
| PGS004698 | CAD | 2.36 |
| PGS004840 | T2D | 1.40 |
| PGS000018 | T2D | 1.05 |
| PGS003897 | BMI | 0.50 |
| PGS002853 | LDL | 0.33 |
| PGS002858 | LDL | 0.30 |
| PGS003092 | BMI | 0.22 |

Extreme shifts included PGS004696 CAD in EAS (δ_EUR = −4.80), PGS004698 CAD in EAS (−4.12), and PGS000014 CAD in AFR (+3.99). Kendall’s W across PGS for population rank concordance was ≈ 0, indicating that different scores reorder populations differently. Distance-sensitivity slopes (score shift vs genetic distance from EUR) were steepest for CAD scores (e.g. PGS000014 slope ≈ 32) and near flat for some BMI/LDL scores.

Interpretation. Cross-ancestry score displacement is large for several CAD/T2D instruments and small for others—portability is score- and trait-specific, not a single genome-wide constant. Near-zero Kendall’s W means one cannot assume a universal “most shifted” population. Because scoring omits chr1–7, absolute score levels are partial-chromosome sums; relative δ_EUR and rank patterns remain informative for the covered autosomes but must not be described as full-genome PRS.

5. Phase 18 intervention

A portability-risk model trained on the 22-chromosome associated set (test AUROC 0.687) was used to filter, reweight, or flag high-risk variants in each PGS, then individuals were re-scored on chr8–22.

`reweight_linear` reduced mean absolute EUR-centered shift for 7 of 9 PGS. The largest improvement was PGS000014 (CAD): MAD 2.85 → 2.48 (reduction 0.37). Two scores (PGS003092 BMI, PGS004840 T2D) did not improve under linear reweighting.

Hard filtering (`filter_10`) was mixed: it improved some scores (e.g. PGS003897, PGS000014) but worsened others, and beat a matched random-drop control for only a subset of PGS. Negative controls (random, FST, LD, MAF filters) show that naive frequency- or LD-based dropping is not equivalent to model-guided intervention.

Per-patient confidence flags (top-decile |weight × dosage| contributions, capped at 20k variants per PGS for runtime) covered all five superpopulations (n = 28,818 person×PGS rows); mean confidence was similar across AFR/AMR/EAS/EUR/SAS (~0.72).

Interpretation. Soft reweighting by predicted portability risk is the more reliable intervention in this setting; aggressive filtering can remove transferable signal along with risky variants. Gains are real but modest and heterogeneous across PGS—consistent with a triage tool, not a universal recalibrator. Confidence flags are descriptive reliability indicators, not calibrated clinical probabilities. All intervention effects are conditional on chr8–22 scoring coverage.

6. Gates and quality control

- `gate_genomewide.py` PASS: 22 chromosomes present; master >10M rows; AF_LD_SEL pooled AUROC > 0.65; per-chromosome mean AUROC ≥ 0.75; permuted controls near 0.5.
- `gate_intervention_genomewide.py` PASS: `reweight_linear` reduces |δ_EUR| for at least one PGS; `filter_10` beats random for at least one PGS; per-patient confidence covers all superpopulations; MAD bootstrap CIs and tail-ancestry metrics present.
- `gate_science_deepen_holdouts.py` PASS: within-trait mean AUROC > pooled; trait-holdout + permutation controls present; holdout headline includes `split_trait`.
- `gate_pathway_enrichment.py` PASS: FDR and leave-one-locus-out tables present for all four traits.

7. Science deepen (post–GW22)

7.1 Generalization and trait stratification

Within-trait AF_LD_SEL models (variant holdout) achieve mean AUROC **0.760** (CAD 0.776, T2D 0.759, BMI 0.755, LDL 0.751), clearly above the pooled 0.683 and with permuted controls near 0.50. Leave-one-trait-out drops to mean **0.616** (CAD/T2D ~0.56; BMI/LDL ~0.66–0.67). Master `split_trait` AUROC is 0.613; `split_source` (panukbb_only train → multisource test) is 0.566.

Interpretation. Population-genetic features predict discordance well *within* a trait, but transfer across traits is limited. That supports reporting trait-specific models as the operational mechanism layer and treating cross-trait generalization as a secondary, weaker claim—not a failure of the atlas.

7.2 Pathway statistics (FDR and LD-aware nulls)

Gene-level Fisher enrichment of high-I² genes, BH-FDR, and 1 Mb LD-block permutation (200 perms) yield: CAD **108** pathways with both q < 0.05; T2D **1**; BMI **1**; LDL **0**. The T2D/BMI survivors are broad (“Signal Transduction”); CAD includes more specific chromatin, Pre-NOTCH, and meiosis pathways. Leave-one-locus-out on top pathways flagged 2/80 as fragile.

Interpretation. Ranked mean-I² pathway tops remain useful hypotheses; FDR+LD filtering sharply reduces claims, especially for T2D/BMI/LDL. Report CAD FDR survivors as the primary pathway result; label other tops hypothesis-generating.

7.3 Expanded intervention metrics

`reweight_linear` MAD reductions are unchanged in point estimate (7/9 PGS improve; largest on PGS000014) and now carry bootstrap CIs. Reweight beats random on MAD reduction for multiple PGS. Top-5% |score| ancestry composition L1 shifts are non-zero for several scores. Sign-concordance on retained variants is unchanged under reweight because all variants are kept (weights only change)—concordance deltas are informative mainly for filter modes.

Interpretation. Soft reweighting remains the preferred intervention; multi-metric evaluation shows MAD gains are not an artifact of a single summary, while concordance is not expected to move under pure reweighting.

8. How to read these results in the paper

1. Lead with the 22-chromosome mechanism table and per-chromosome replication; use pooled AUROC as a conservative headline and per-chr / within-trait means as the stronger local claims.
2. State explicitly that trait-holdout is weaker (~0.56–0.67): portability risk is partly trait-specific.
3. Present pathway tops with claim tiers: FDR+LD significant (mainly CAD) vs hypothesis-generating.
4. Report the score-shift atlas with explicit chr8–22 genotype coverage; treat CAD/T2D large δ_EUR as the main Q1 finding.
5. Frame Phase 18 as proof-of-concept triage: emphasize `reweight_linear` (7/9 improved, CIs, beats random) and negative controls.
6. Keep partial16 and chr22 as sensitivity analyses.
7. Do not claim full-autosome PRS sums or clinical utility.

Open follow-on. Restore chr1–7 score pgens from VCF to extend atlas and intervention to full autosomes; until then, all PRS-level claims remain chr8–22.
9. Literature roadmap results (Phases A-D)

9.1 MHC sensitivity

On the MHC-aware associated sample (n~508k; 58,081 MHC rows), AF_LD_SEL AUROC was 0.735 pooled, 0.720 with MHC excluded, 0.850 MHC-only, and 0.735 with an is_mhc indicator. MHC is enriched for high-I2 labels (test positive rate 0.65 vs 0.38 outside) and is highly predictable from AF/LD/SEL features; excluding MHC lowers AUROC only modestly (~0.015). MHC is therefore not silently driving the non-MHC claim, but MHC-only performance should be reported separately for autoimmune follow-ons.

9.2 LD-block CV and baselines (primary CV)

| Split | AF_LD_SEL | FST | POP_DISTANCE | RG_PROXY |
|-------|-----------|-----|--------------|----------|
| split_variant | 0.730 | 0.587 | 0.673 | 0.702 |
| split_ld_block | 0.627 | 0.579 | 0.617 | 0.618 |

LD-block holdout drops AF_LD_SEL from 0.730 to 0.627 (expected leakage reduction). AF_LD_SEL still beats FST, POP_DISTANCE, and RG_PROXY under both splits. Use 0.627 as the primary CV headline for the paper; variant-holdout remains sensitivity. (Sample protocol; full-master pooled remains 0.683.)

9.3 Fine-map tiers

Heuristic tiers (LD-block lead I2): tag_only AUROC 0.733; pooled sample 0.730. Fine_mapped AUROC 0.796 is not interpretable under the current heuristic because lead-by-I2 nearly coincides with y_high_I2 (test positive rate ~0.996). Treat fine-mapped vs tag-only as a methods scaffold until SuSiE CS labels replace the heuristic.

9.4 VEP x AF interaction

LOEUF/pLI proxy x AF divergence did not improve over AF_LD_SEL on the common-EUR stratum (0.733 vs 0.730); rare-EUR stratum AUROC was lower (0.647), consistent with sparser signal / harder labels rather than a clear VEP-bias recovery. AlphaMissense join remains optional when the GRCh38 parquet is present.

9.5 Duffy positive control

rs2814778 +/- 200 kb mean predicted risk was not elevated vs matched null windows (z~-0.11; gate FAIL). Expected without WBC in the trait panel; re-run after WBC labels are wired.

